// @check
// @error `u8` is a storage type, not a value type — a `u8[]` stores the low byte of an `i32`
// @error `u8` is a storage type, not a value type — a `u8[]` stores the low byte of an `i32`
// @error `u8` is a storage type, not a value type — a `u8[]` stores the low byte of an `i32`
// @error `u8` is a storage type, not a value type — a `u8[]` stores the low byte of an `i32`
// @error unknown type `Bogus` in `as` cast
// D1583 — `x as u8` USED TO SAY `unknown type \`u8\``, which is the one thing that is not
// true: `u8[]` is a spellable type and `std:fs`'s `readFile` answers one, so the name
// clearly names something. It names a STORAGE type — the element position exists, the
// VALUE position does not (`error-u8-storage-only.vl`) — so there is no cast to write
// and none is needed: the store already truncates to the low byte
// (`u8-packed-list.vl`, `error-u8-literal-range.vl`). Reported by the glean session,
// VL-037, while formatting a Warcraft III four-character type id.
//
// Four positions, one message: a `push` argument, a list-literal element, an index
// write, and a bare binding. The remedy differs from the ANNOTATION position's, which is
// why this is its own sentence rather than `storageTyLeafFail`'s: there the answer is
// "write `i32`", here it is "write nothing".
//
// `as Bogus` is the CONTROL — the arm is gated on the NAME, so an ordinary unknown type
// in a cast keeps its own wording. It is also what pins the arm as reachable only when
// the name did not resolve, so a program declaring its own `type u8` is unaffected.
const v = 300

const bytes: u8[] = []
bytes.push(v as u8)

const lit: u8[] = [v as u8]

const w: u8[] = [0]
w[0] = v as u8

const bare = v as u8

const other = v as Bogus

// The two spellings the message names, both legal, both storing 44.
const ok: u8[] = []
ok.push(v)
ok.push(v & 0xff)
print(bytes.length + lit.length + w.length + ok.length + bare + other)
